#{[:tutorial/id #uuid "0f97993b-9e3a-5621-807f-4cb2646d6ab5" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761910 :tutorial/content "**MTH 256 — Recitation 7: Quiz 3 Review**\n\nDue: unknown\nDate imported: 2026-11-13\nSource: [MTH 256 F 2026 Recitation 7 Quiz 3 Review.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F256%2FR-7%2FMTH%20256%20F%202026%20Recitation%207%20Quiz%203%20Review.pdf)\nAnswer key: [MTH 256 F 2026 Recitation 7 Quiz 3 Review Key.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F256%2FR-7%2FMTH%20256%20F%202026%20Recitation%207%20Quiz%203%20Review%20Key.pdf)\n\nStudy answers and explanations are authored additions, independently checked against the downloaded key. Problems 1 and 10 contain source inconsistencies; their original wording is preserved and the verified conclusions are explained in the answer reveals. The instructor's intended corrections remain unconfirmed. Neither PDF specifies a due date.\n\n## Instructions\n\nThe recitation this week serves as a review for quiz three, which will be taken in the latter half of recitation time. It also prepares you for the wording on the quiz. The quiz covers Sections 5.1 and 5.2 and Euler equations from Section 7.4. The following definition and theorems will be provided on the quiz (source PDF p. 1).\n\n### Wronskian definition\n\nFor two functions $\\{y_1(x),y_2(x)\\}$,\n$$W(x)=y_1y_2'-y_1'y_2.$$\n\n**Topics:** [Linear Independence of Solutions to Homogeneous ODEs](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.1.%20Theory%20of%20Solutions%20to%20Linear%20ODEs/Lessons/3.1.6.%20Linear%20Independence%20of%20Solutions%20to%20Homogeneous%20ODEs.md>).\n**Prerequisites:** No additional prerequisite links needed.\n\n### Theorem 5.2.1 — Constant coefficients\n\nLet $p(r)=ar^2+br+c$ be the characteristic polynomial of\n$$ay''+by'+cy=0.\\tag{5.2.20}$$\n\n(a) If $p(r)=0$ has distinct real roots $r_1,r_2$, then the general solution is\n$$y=c_1e^{r_1x}+c_2e^{r_2x}.$$\n\n(b) If it has a repeated root $r_1$, then the general solution is\n$$y=e^{r_1x}(c_1+c_2x).$$\n\n(c) If it has complex conjugate roots $r_1=\\lambda+i\\omega$ and $r_2=\\lambda-i\\omega$, where $\\omega>0$, then the general solution is\n$$y=e^{\\lambda x}(c_1\\cos\\omega x+c_2\\sin\\omega x).$$\n\n**Topics:** [Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equations With Distinct Real Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.2.%20Second-Order%20Linear%20ODEs%20With%20Constant%20Coefficients/Lessons/3.2.1.%20Second-Order%20Homogeneous%20ODEs-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Distinct%20Real%20Roots.md>); [Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equations With Repeated Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.2.%20Second-Order%20Linear%20ODEs%20With%20Constant%20Coefficients/Lessons/3.2.2.%20Second-Order%20Homogeneous%20ODEs-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Repeated%20Roots.md>); [Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equations With Complex Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.2.%20Second-Order%20Linear%20ODEs%20With%20Constant%20Coefficients/Lessons/3.2.3.%20Second-Order%20Homogeneous%20ODEs-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Complex%20Roots.md>).\n**Prerequisites:** No additional prerequisite links needed.\n\n### Theorem 7.4.3 — Euler equations\n\nSuppose the roots of the indicial equation\n$$ar(r-1)+br+c=0\\tag{7.4.11}$$\nare $r_1,r_2$. Then the general solution of the Euler equation\n$$ax^2y''+bxy'+cy=0\\tag{7.4.12}$$\non $(0,\\infty)$ is\n\n- $y=c_1x^{r_1}+c_2x^{r_2}$ if $r_1,r_2$ are distinct real numbers;\n- $y=x^{r_1}(c_1+c_2\\ln x)$ if $r_1=r_2$;\n- $y=x^\\lambda[c_1\\cos(\\omega\\ln x)+c_2\\sin(\\omega\\ln x)]$ if $r_1,r_2=\\lambda\\pm i\\omega$ with $\\omega>0$.\n\n**Topics:** [Cauchy-Euler Equations: Characteristic Equations With Distinct Real Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.3.%20The%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations/Lessons/3.3.1.%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Distinct%20Real%20Roots.md>); [Cauchy-Euler Equations: Characteristic Equations With Repeated Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.3.%20The%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations/Lessons/3.3.2.%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Repeated%20Roots.md>); [Cauchy-Euler Equations: Characteristic Equations With Complex Roots](<file:///home/jake/Developer/study/MA/Mathematical-Analysis-%26-Modeling/DEQ/3.%20Linear%20Differential%20Equations/3.3.%20The%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations/Lessons/3.3.3.%20Cauchy-Euler%20Equations-%20Characteristic%20Equations%20With%20Complex%20Roots.md>).\n**Prerequisites:** No additional prerequisite links needed." :tutorial/id #uuid "0f97993b-9e3a-5621-807f-4cb2646d6ab5" :tutorial/title "Assignment information"}] [:question/id #uuid "228f22c4-9d80-5d95-bd19-73b3f5bad258" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df1ec775-25c8-5177-a130-e1547608717a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "C_1x+C_2x^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761950}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761950} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a6be089-872c-5fc5-8863-8711b543e132" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761923}] :question/id #uuid "228f22c4-9d80-5d95-bd19-73b3f5bad258" :question/problem "Source question: PDF p. 5, Problem 10.\n\nThe source states that the indicial equation\n$$r^2+2r-3=0$$\nfor the Euler equation\n$$y''+3xy'-3y=0$$\nhas solutions $r_1=-1$ and $r_2=3$. Find the general solution to\n$$x^2y''+3xy'-3y=0.$$\n\n**Source conflict:** The first displayed ODE omits the $x^2$ multiplying $y''$, and the stated roots do not solve the printed indicial equation. For the study answer, solve the final displayed ODE on $(0,\\infty)$, using the Euler-theorem interval from page 1.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{answer}}." :question/worked-solution "Substitute $y=x^r$ into the final ODE to get $r(r-1)+3r-3=r^2+2r-3=(r-1)(r+3)=0$. Its actual roots are $1$ and $-3$, so $y=C_1x+C_2x^{-3}$ on $(0,\\infty)$. Each term has residual zero in the final ODE.\n\nThe key follows the erroneous stated roots and gives $C_1x^{-1}+C_2x^3$. These terms have residuals $-4x^{-1}$ and $12x^3$, respectively, so that expression is not the general solution of the final printed equation. It would instead solve $x^2y''-xy'-3y=0$. The original wording is retained above; no instructor correction has been assumed."}] [:question/id #uuid "3b51ca7a-7572-534e-b8b8-82d1a8af6ccb" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d27c13c3-fb87-56cd-b9aa-d267ea5e9f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The requested conclusion is false for the printed ODE. With $y_1=x^{-3}$ and $y_2=x$, the derivatives are $y_1'=-3x^{-4}$ and $y_2'=1$, so\n$$W=y_1y_2'-y_1'y_2=x^{-3}-(-3x^{-4})x=4x^{-3}\\ne0\\quad(x>0).$$\nThus the functions are linearly independent. But for $L[y]=x^2y''+4xy'-y$,\n$$L[x^{-3}]=12x^{-3}-12x^{-3}-x^{-3}=-x^{-3},\\qquad L[x]=4x-x=3x.$$\nNeither function solves the printed equation, so they cannot be its fundamental set.\n\nThe key instead prints $x^2y''+3xy'-y=0$; its residuals are $2x^{-3}$ and $2x$, so that version also fails. The equation $x^2y''+3xy'-3y=0$ does have these two solutions, and the same nonzero Wronskian proves they form a fundamental set on $(0,\\infty)$. This last equation is an authored possible correction, not a confirmed instructor correction." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761948}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186761948} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd0ff44b-2295-53ff-a2de-b5a8b8c914e1" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761946}] :question/id #uuid "3b51ca7a-7572-534e-b8b8-82d1a8af6ccb" :question/problem "Source question: PDF p. 2, Problem 1.\n\nThe set of functions $\\{x^{-3},x\\}$ solve\n$$x^2y''+4xy'-y=0.$$\nShow that $\\{x^{-3},x\\}$ form a fundamental set of solutions for $0<x<\\infty$. Your solution must include a Wronskian calculation.\n\n**Source conflict:** The worksheet and key print different equations here. The assertion is inconsistent with both printed versions. Preserve the requested Wronskian work and explain what the calculation actually establishes." :question/worked-solution "A nonzero Wronskian proves independence; membership in the ODE's solution set must also hold. The key's Wronskian is correct, but it does not establish the claimed solution property. The instructor's intended correction remains unconfirmed."}] [:answer/id #uuid "d27c13c3-fb87-56cd-b9aa-d267ea5e9f8f" {:answer/id #uuid "d27c13c3-fb87-56cd-b9aa-d267ea5e9f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The requested conclusion is false for the printed ODE. With $y_1=x^{-3}$ and $y_2=x$, the derivatives are $y_1'=-3x^{-4}$ and $y_2'=1$, so\n$$W=y_1y_2'-y_1'y_2=x^{-3}-(-3x^{-4})x=4x^{-3}\\ne0\\quad(x>0).$$\nThus the functions are linearly independent. But for $L[y]=x^2y''+4xy'-y$,\n$$L[x^{-3}]=12x^{-3}-12x^{-3}-x^{-3}=-x^{-3},\\qquad L[x]=4x-x=3x.$$\nNeither function solves the printed equation, so they cannot be its fundamental set.\n\nThe key instead prints $x^2y''+3xy'-y=0$; its residuals are $2x^{-3}$ and $2x$, so that version also fails. The equation $x^2y''+3xy'-3y=0$ does have these two solutions, and the same nonzero Wronskian proves they form a fundamental set on $(0,\\infty)$. This last equation is an authored possible correction, not a confirmed instructor correction." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186761948}]}
